text:
Tom (AE): Marta filed a POC bug — their webapp crashes sporadically when rendering streamed completions. Browser shows `Uncaught SyntaxError: Unexpected token \u0000` during JSON.parse. Happens only on longer generations.
Marta (Client): repro: open SSE to /v1/stream, after ~5 events one event contains a NUL and our client attempt to JSON.parse the data and throws. No 5xx from origin.
Eli (SE): can you capture the raw bytes from the browser-visible path? `curl -N -v https://poc.example.com/v1/stream > stream.bin` then `xxd -g1 stream.bin | sed -n '1,160p'` — paste the region around the failure.
Marta (Client): pasted hexdump excerpt we saw: `... 7d 0a 0a 64 61 74 61 3a 20 7b 22msg\":\"long part\"7d 00 0a 0a` — there's a 00 between the JSON end and the blank line.
Jun (Oncall): 0x00 inside SSE payload will break simple JSON parsers. What's in front of your origin? Any LBs/proxies/CDN?
Marta (Client): GCLB (Google Cloud Load Balancer) -> haproxy -> nginx -> app.
Ravi (Eng): interesting stack. We've seen issues when an HTTP/2 terminator or LB does HTTP/2->HTTP/1.1 conversions and re-chunks responses; sometimes padding zeros slip in (esp. with keepalive pooling or buffer reuse). Hapoxy custom buffering or a misconfigured timeout can also leave stale bytes.
Eli (SE): additional debug steps: 1) curl -N directly to origin (bypass GCLB/haproxy) if possible, 2) run `tcpdump -s 0 -w raw.pcap host app-ip and port 443` and inspect the raw bytes from origin->client path, 3) compare hex slices.
Marta (Client): I bypassed haproxy (direct to GCLB) and still saw 0x00 in the capture. Bypassed GCLB -> direct to origin, no 0x00. So edge LB is the likely culprint.
Jun (Oncall): OK, so something at the LB layer is inserting padding. Quick mitigations: - Ensure streaming response uses Transfer-Encoding: chunked and no Content-Length. - Disable any response buffering or response normalization at the LB/CDN layer for the streaming route. For GCLB check backend service settings and any URL map policies that might enable response buffering. For haproxy, ensure `option http-buffer-request off` and try `nbproc` settings? (we'll give exact snippet).
Eli (SE): client-side hardening (short-term): don't attempt to JSON.parse per incoming chunk. Accumulate event frames until the SSE separator is observed. Also defensively strip NULs as a temporary fallback. Example pattern (browser-friendly):
```
let buffer = '';
const es = new EventSource('/v1/stream');
es.onmessage = (e) => {
  // if streaming via raw fetch, accumulate manually; EventSource should give you whole events
};
// If you use fetch/streams
const reader = resp.body.getReader();
async function pump(){
  while(true){
    const {value,done} = await reader.read();
    if(done) break;
    buffer += new TextDecoder().decode(value, {stream:true});
    let sep;
    while((sep = buffer.indexOf('\n\n')) !== -1){
      const evt = buffer.slice(0,sep).trim();
      buffer = buffer.slice(sep+2);
      const dataLines = evt.split('\n').filter(l => l.startsWith('data:'));
      const payload = dataLines.map(l => l.replace(/^data:\s*/,'')).join('\n');
      const cleaned = payload.replace(/\u0000/g,'');
      try{ handle(JSON.parse(cleaned)); } catch(e){ console.warn('sse parse fail', e); }
    }
  }
}
```
Marta (Client): we updated client to accumulate and strip \u0000. That removed the crash but we want to fix root cause.
Ravi (Eng): on Redwood side we can temporarily flip a tenant runtime flag `padless_sse=true` that forces explicit flushes per event and avoids internal buffer reuse that can leave trailing NULLs. Low risk for POC but we need approval to change tenant runtime behavior.
Nora (Presales): fine to toggle for troubleshooting, just document it in the POC notes and flag for rollback after POC.
Jun (Oncall): I'll dump haproxy config for the streaming frontend and suggest changes. For haproxy try setting in the frontend/backend for the stream path: `option http-server-close`, `timeout http-request 5s`, `timeout http-keep-alive 300s`, and disable any response buffering or compression for that route. Also ensure `tune.bufsize` isn't being misused with reused buffer objects.
Eli (SE): GCLB specifics: disable CDN caching/edge response buffering for the path — check BackendService 'enable_cdn' and Cloud CDN policies. If using HTTP/2 between client and load balancer, try setting `HTTP2->ON` or off to see behavior differences; some HTTP/2 terminators reframe frames and can add padding.
Marta (Client): we've toggled GCLB to disable response normalization and set haproxy to `option http-server-close` and `http-no-delay` for the stream path. Re-captured with curl -N -> no NULs seen in 6 runs. Browser still got one stale NUL earlier but it's greatly reduced.
Ravi (Eng): follow-ups: 1) Jun to publish haproxy + GCLB stream checklist into the POC doc, 2) I will enable `padless_sse=true` for this tenant for the duration of the POC and log occurrences, 3) Eli/Marta to run traffic over longer durations and report any remaining anomalies.
infra-bot: Deployed `poc/haproxy-gclb-sse-fix` to staging (build #1588).
Eli (SE): also recommend checking app server write patterns — if you use a pooling buffer (e.g., reusing a ByteBuffer) ensure it's zeroed or you write exact lengths, and avoid `sendfile` for dynamic streams.
Marta (Client): after the infra changes + client accumulator + runtime toggle we've had 72h with no crashes in staging. We'll plan a gradual rollout to prod edge with canaries.
Jun (Oncall): I'll also run a grep across our edge fleet for any evidence of NUL bytes in streamed responses and open follow-ups if we find other domains.
Ravi (Eng): I'll add an internal ticket to consider making `padless_sse` default for new POCs and to add telemetry buckets for padding bytes per-tenant.
Tom (AE): great work — please add the haproxy/GCLB config snippets and the client accumulator code to the POC wrap-up for the customer. Nora, please include runtime toggle in the next-step checklist.
Nora (Presales): will do and will note the temporary nature of the runtime flag.
Marta (Client): thanks everyone, big improvement. Closing this thread after docs are linked. :trophy:
